.TH SC 1 "2026-10-02" "sc-fleet 0.1.0" "sc-fleet manual"
.SH NAME
sc \- the spacecowboy fleet: one namespace over a set of small tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBsc\fR
\fBsc ls\fR
\fBsc logo\fR
\fBsc\fR \fItool\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsc\fR is a dispatcher and an identity, not a framework. It gives a set of
independent shell tools one namespace, one palette, one output grammar, and
one man page each.
.PP
Running a tool through \fBsc\fR is identical to calling it directly:
\fBsc wt ls\fR and \fBwt ls\fR are the same binary with the same arguments.
The namespace exists so the fleet is discoverable, not so it is mandatory.
.SH MEMBERSHIP
Membership is not a manifest, which would only drift. A tool is a member
when it
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
lives in the same directory as \fBsc\fR (not in \fIlib/\fR),
.IP \(bu 2
is executable,
.IP \(bu 2
carries a line beginning \fB# sc-fleet\fR, and
.IP \(bu 2
answers the hidden \fB__id\fR verb.
.RE
.PP
The marker is checked by reading the file, \fIbefore\fR anything is executed,
so \fBsc\fR never runs a neighbouring script merely to ask what it is.
.PP
Because membership is derived rather than listed, dropping a conforming tool
into the directory is the whole of installation:
\fBinstall.sh\fR reads the same marker to decide what to link
onto \fBPATH\fR and into the man path.
.PP
Members are looked for in the directory \fBsc\fR lives in and then in
\fBSC_BIN_EXTRA\fR (default \fI~/.local/bin\fR), one entry per real path,
so a symlink to a tool already seen is not a second tool. That is how a tool
kept in another repository joins: \fBdye\fR lives in the dots, carries the
marker, and reaches the roster, the menu, the completion and \fBsc dye\fR
through its \fI~/.local/bin\fR link. Tools the fleet expects on every box
are still only those in \fBsc\fR's own directory; \fBrig\fR reads that.
.SH THE CONTRACT A TOOL SIGNS
A member sets four facts and sources the shared kit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
SC_TOOL='wt'                          # <= 7 chars: sc ls pads to %-7s
SC_TAGLINE='one worktree per topic'
SC_MOTTO='every checkout a sibling'
SC_SIGIL=$' \(rs\(rsu256d\(rs\(rsu25c6 \(rsn...'   # 3 rows, each padded to 4 CHARACTERS
\&. "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/lib/sckit.sh"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sigil rows are padded to four \fIcharacters\fR by hand because bash pads
field widths by bytes, and every glyph in the fleet is multibyte.
.PP
\fB__id\fR prints six tab-separated fields on one line: name, tagline, motto,
and the three sigil rows. Tab separated so the rows keep their padding.
.PP
\fBThe kit must be optional.\fR Each tool sources \fIlib/sckit.sh\fR inside a
conditional and provides its own fallbacks, so a single file copied to a bare
box still runs. The test suites enforce this by copying a tool alone into a
directory with no \fIlib/\fR and asserting \fB--help\fR still exits 0.
.SH OUTPUT GRAMMAR
The kit supplies the shared vocabulary: \fBrow\fR (a 9-wide dim label, a
42-wide value, a free-form note), \fBok\fR, \fBbad\fR and \fBdim\fR for the
note column, \fBwarn\fR, and \fBdie\fR, which prints \fBrefuse\fR to stderr
and exits 1. Width budget is 72 columns.
.PP
Refusals name the offending fact and then the command that fixes it.
.SS Art
A fixed alphabet, so a diagram means the same thing in every tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\(em heavy    durable: a clone, a store, a trunk
\(em light    ephemeral: a topic, a worktree, a shell
\(em dashed   gone: pruned, deleted
\(bu landed   \(di yours   \(ci elsewhere
.fi
.RE
.PP
Glyphs are drawn only for a human: never into a pipe, never in
\fB--porcelain\fR output, and off entirely with \fI<TOOL>\fB_NO_ART\fR. At the
same precedence "off" beats "on", so \fBSC_NO_ART=1\fR in CI cannot be undone
by a stray \fBSC_ART\fR elsewhere.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS sc
At a terminal with \fBfzf\fR, the menu under the logo: the tools first, each
previewing its verbs, then the chosen tool's verbs to pick from. Through a
pipe, or without fzf, the logo, the roster and the usage. See THE MENU.
.SS sc \fItool\fR
A member named alone opens the menu filtered to that tool's verbs, rather
than running the tool bare. Most tools do something on a bare invocation;
from \fBsc\fR, the menu is the safer default.
.SS sc ls
The roster alone: one line per member, its middle sigil row and its tagline.
.SS sc logo
The full banner, drawn even into a pipe, for pasting into a README: a night in
the desert (a ringed planet, the moon, a saguaro, the cowboy and his terminal)
above the name, coloured in the worn theme's roles at a terminal and plain
ASCII through a pipe.
.SS sc \fItool\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
Run a fleet tool. Refuses a name that is not a member, and names \fBsc ls\fR.
.PP
A shell function of the same name takes precedence when one exists, because
some subcommands can only act inside the calling shell: \fBsc wt cd\fR and
\fBsc arm\fR would otherwise run in a child process and change nothing. See
\fBfleet.zsh\fR.
.SH THE MENU
Derived, like the roster: no list to drift. Every member declares its verbs
in its header block in one grammar,
.PP
.RS
.nf
#   rig doctor [--disk]    what is missing or misconfigured here
.fi
.RE
.PP
and its own \fBusage()\fR prints those lines. The menu parses the same
lines: the verb plus anything that looks like arguments is the command, what
follows is the description, a trailing \fB# text\fR counts as the
description too (\fBwt\fR's convention), and a deeper-indented next line
continues the entry. A verb is in the menu the moment its header line exists.
.PP
Two levels, both under the logo. The first lists the tools with their
taglines and previews the highlighted tool's verbs on the right; Enter opens
that tool's verbs. Escape there goes back to the tools. The full scene needs
forty rows above a list, so on a shorter terminal the wordmark stands in and
the logo is still there.
.PP
Enter runs a flag-only verb as picked (\fBrig doctor\fR, \fBwt ls\fR). A
verb that needs a value, a required \fI<slug>\fR or a bracketed VALUE word
such as \fB[-m MSG]\fR, lands on the prompt instead with the cursor at the
end, to be finished and sent. Tab forces the prompt for any entry, which is
how you add an optional flag or look before you leap. Escape cancels.
.PP
The menu wears the theme through \fBFZF_DEFAULT_OPTS\fR, which \fBdye\fR
renders.
.PP
The split of labour: the binary derives the entries (\fBsc __menu\fR, TSV of
tool, command, description, needs-args) and runs fzf (\fBsc __pick\fR); the
shell function in \fBfleet.zsh\fR is what can run a shell function such as
\fBwt cd\fR or bare \fBarm\fR, or place text on your prompt, so the
decision to run or prefill lives there. A pick's exit is 0, a cancel 1, and
"no menu here" 2, on which the function shows the splash instead.
.SH THE FLEET
.TP
.B wt
One worktree per topic, across projects and forges.
.TP
.B arm
Re-arm running shells with the current kit.
.TP
.B crew
Every Claude session you have running, and what it is doing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/sc
The dispatcher, in the sc-fleet checkout. It carries no \fB# sc-fleet\fR marker of its own and excludes
itself from the roster by name.
.TP
.I lib/sckit.sh
The shared kit: palette, art helpers, output vocabulary, \fBsc_id\fR.
.TP
.I lib/mkart.py
Regenerates the logo block in the kit from its drawing: ASCII sprites, each
character tagged with a palette role. Run by hand at authoring
time; nothing calls it at run time.
.TP
.I shell/fleet.zsh
The fleet's shell surface: functions and completion wiring, sourced once at
startup and again by \fBarm\fR.
.TP
.I tests/fixtures/voidrunner.json
The palette the kit's built-in colours must agree with. \fBtests/test_sc.sh\fR fails if they
drift apart.
.TP
.I tests/test_sc.sh
The test suite. Run: \fBbash tests/test_sc.sh\fR
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SC_BIN_EXTRA
Space-separated directories searched for members after \fBsc\fR's own.
Default \fI~/.local/bin\fR; set empty to search only the fleet's directory.
.TP
.B SC_W
Width budget. Default 72.
.TP
.B SC_ART
Force art even into a pipe, which is what makes banners testable.
.TP
.B SC_NO_ART \fRor\fB NO_COLOR
Suppress art, or colour. Per-tool \fI<TOOL>\fB_ART\fR and
\fI<TOOL>\fB_NO_ART\fR override these; the most specific setting wins.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on a refusal, with a \fBrefuse\fR line on stderr. A dispatched
tool's status passes through unchanged, because \fBsc\fR \fBexec\fRs it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wt (1),
.BR arm (1),
.BR crew (1)
